# scene-level defaults: camera rig, background, and render hints.
#
# Camera is the only "special" mesh — the renderer treats it as the view
# transform rather than drawable geometry. Every scene has exactly one
# camera leader, typically bound via `camera = DEFAULT_CAMERA`.

# constructors

# build a camera from a position, a target point, and an up vector.
## [overview]
## Build a perspective camera from a position, target, up vector, and clipping range.
## [description]
## The value is a tagged map consumed specially by the renderer. Animate the camera leader with `CameraLerp` rather than plain `Lerp` for smoother up-vector motion.
## [parameters]
## up: preferred screen-up direction; it should not be parallel to the view direction
## near: near clipping plane distance
## far: far clipping plane distance
## fov: vertical field of view in radians, strictly between 0 and PI (default about 58.7 degrees)
## [example]
## ```
## camera = Camera(position: [2, 1, 5], target: ORIGIN)
## camera = Camera(position: [0, 0, 6], fov: deg_to_rad(30))
## play CameraLerp(&camera, 1)
## ```
let Camera = |position = 4b, target = [0,0,0], up = 1u, near = 0.1, far = 100, fov = 1.0247789| __monocurl__native__ mk_camera(position, target, up, near, far, fov)

## [overview]
## Build an orthographic camera from a position, target, up vector, and clipping range.
## [description]
## Parallel projection: objects keep the same on-screen size regardless of their distance from the camera. `height` is the world-space height of the visible region, and it applies at every depth. Like `Camera`, the value is consumed specially by the renderer, and `CameraLerp` interpolates between cameras of the same kind.
## [parameters]
## up: preferred screen-up direction; it should not be parallel to the view direction
## near: near clipping plane distance
## far: far clipping plane distance
## height: world-space height of the visible region
## [example]
## ```
## camera = OrthographicCamera(position: [0, 0, 5], height: 8)
## ```
let OrthographicCamera = |position = 4b, target = [0,0,0], up = 1u, near = 0.1, far = 100, height = 8| __monocurl__native__ mk_orthographic_camera(position, target, up, near, far, height)


# defaults
# these two names are conventional — `camera = DEFAULT_CAMERA` /
# `background = DEFAULT_BACKGROUND` give you a sensible starting scene.

## [overview]
## Initial camera used by new scenes.
## [description]
## Equivalent to a camera at `[0, 0, 4]`, looking at the origin with `UP` as the up vector.
## [example]
## ```
## camera = DEFAULT_CAMERA
## ```
let DEFAULT_CAMERA = __monocurl__native__ initial_camera()
## [overview]
## Initial background color used by new scenes.
## [description]
## A solid white background value. Assign `background = BLACK` or another RGBA color/map to change it.
## [example]
## ```
## background = DEFAULT_BACKGROUND
## ```
let DEFAULT_BACKGROUND = __monocurl__native__ initial_background()


# render context

## [overview]
## Current viewport aspect ratio.
## [description]
## Returns the active execution aspect ratio as `width / height`. Preview presets, presentation, and export settings update this value before the scene is evaluated.
## [example]
## ```
## let frame_w = 4 * aspect_ratio()
## ```
let aspect_ratio = || __monocurl__native__ aspect_ratio()
